;;; misc-uncategorized.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census key: misc-uncategorized   ·   family: language   ·   counted-real (catch-all)

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — misc-uncategorized, the normalizer's floor")

(error-card :key "misc-uncategorized"
  :name "Miscellaneous / uncategorized"
  :family "language"
  :census-status "counted-real"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/misc-uncategorized.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"Not a Scheme category — a NORMALIZER category. It is the last bucket
`normalizeError` falls into: the thrown thing matched no language-family regex,
had no `namespace/name:` verb prefix, and carried no distinguishing class name.
The census must count SOMETHING, so it counts `misc-uncategorized`."

  :trigger-code "(hash-table-ref (make-hash-table) (quote k))"
  :observed-output "error: hash-table-ref: key not found: k"
  :normalizes-to "misc-uncategorized"

  :meaning
"This key is the DUST BIN of the census — and reading it well is a real skill.
A message lands here for one of two reasons:

  1. It is a genuine, specific error whose message simply lacks the shape the
     normalizer routes on. `hash-table-ref: key not found: k` is a perfectly
     clear error — but its verb name `hash-table-ref` has no `/` (the verb-prefix
     regex requires `namespace/name:`), and its wording matches no family, so it
     falls through to misc.

  2. It is a truly novel failure the roster has never seen — the honest 'I don't
     have a category for this yet' case.

A rising `misc-uncategorized` count is therefore a WORK QUEUE, not a mystery: it
says 'errors are escaping that the census cannot name'. Each one is a candidate
either to give a `namespace/name:` prefix (so it routes to a per-verb key) or to
add a new family regex (so it routes to a language key)."

  :common-causes (
    "a verb whose error message uses a bare name without a `/` — `hash-table-ref:
     key not found`, `list-ref: index out of range` (if worded without the family
     phrase)"
    "an internal assertion with author wording that matches no family"
    "a brand-new failure mode nobody has carded yet")

  :at-the-verb
"Read the message literally — misc errors are usually SELF-DESCRIBING even though
the census could not classify them (`hash-table-ref: key not found: k` tells you
exactly what happened). The verb name is at the head of the message; the problem
is right after the colon. The census failing to name it does not make the error
vague — it makes the error UNCOUNTED-BY-KIND, which is a documentation gap, not a
debugging one."

  :how-to-detect
"Catch it and read the message string; misc errors carry their meaning in the
text even when the key is generic:"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(display
  (guard (e (#t (error-object-message e)))
    (hash-table-ref (make-hash-table) 'k)))"
  :how-to-detect-output "hash-table-ref: key not found: k"

  :fix-shape
"For the CODE: do what the message says — supply a default (`hash-table-ref/default`
or the 3-arg form), or check `hash-table-contains?` before the ref. For the
LANGUAGE: if this misc key keeps recurring, promote it — either reword the verb's
error to `namespace/name: ...` so it routes to a per-verb census key, or add a
family regex + a real error-card. That promotion is exactly 'the system for the
production of more': misc is where new cards are born."

  :lesson
"`misc-uncategorized` is the census being honest about its own limits: it is the
`I don't know what to call this` bucket. Treat it as a to-do list. Most entries
are clear errors wearing a generic label; a few are genuinely new. Draining this
bucket — by prefixing verb messages and adding families — is how the error
taxonomy grows to match reality instead of pretending it already does."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-count-of \"misc-uncategorized\")"
    "(stats/error-counts 20)"
    "(stats/error-novel)")

  :related-cards ("class-ErrorObject" "not-a-procedure" "roster-not-observed"))
